reed to standardize on negative ground. **Does it matter today?** For your daily commute? Not at all. Where it *does* matter is if you ever buy, restore, or try to jump-start a classic car. If you try to jump-start a 1953 Ford using a modern Toyota, and you hook the cables up "normally" (positive to positive, negative to negative), you will create a dead short, resulting in melted cables, destroyed batteries, and potentially a fire. Similarly, if you try to wire a modern Bluetooth radio into a 1965 MG without converting the car's polarity first, the radio will instantly go up in smoke. So, you are entirely correct that in the modern world, it's a settled debate. But it took decades of engineering trial and error for the industry to finally agree that negative ground was the best way to build a car!
